{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "Explain Bayes' rule in your own words, correctly compute the posterior probability in a changed medical-test example with a different base rate and sensitivity, and show that you no longer swap the base rate with the sensitivity."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "MASTERY",
          "outcome": "Develop the ability to explain Bayes' rule and apply it to a changed medical-test probability example, without confusing the base rate with sensitivity.",
          "explanation": "The reported weakness is a specific confusion: swapping the base rate (prevalence) with sensitivity. The smallest useful strategy is one integrated activity that (a) explains the rule during the work, (b) solves the actual changed example with correct numbers and checks, and (c) directly targets the confusion by requiring an explicit statement of which quantity is which. No separate relearning set or second full check is needed unless the integrated work actually fails; a bounded contingent alternative is provided for that case. The scope is small and the outcome is one changed example plus explanation, so a single committed activity with a 60-minute timebox is sufficient and respects the comfortable pacing while not spreading work across unnecessary sessions.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 40,
            "likelyMinutes": 55,
            "highMinutes": 75
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_core",
              "title": "Explain and apply Bayes' rule to a changed example",
              "outcome": "A written explanation of Bayes' rule and a correctly computed posterior for a changed medical-test example, with explicit identification of base rate versus sensitivity."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Learner identifies the base rate (prevalence) and distinguishes it from sensitivity in the changed example, directly addressing the reported confusion."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Learner states Bayes' rule and uses it to compute the posterior probability in the changed example, showing each term."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The changed medical-test example with different base rate and sensitivity serves as the transfer/application task that demonstrates the capability."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "bayes_changed_example",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_core"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Explain Bayes' rule and solve a changed medical-test probability example",
            "activityType": "Integrated explanation and application",
            "purpose": "Produce a written explanation of Bayes' rule and a correct posterior computation on a changed example, explicitly distinguishing base rate from sensitivity so the reported confusion is directly addressed.",
            "instructions": "Work through this in one sitting, writing your reasoning as you go. Use only the probability methods you have already been taught (Bayes' rule and basic conditional probability); do not use unfamiliar advanced techniques. If you try an unfamiliar method, treat it as optional exploration and return to the taught method for the required answer.\n\n1. Write Bayes' rule for a medical test in your own words: P(disease | positive) = P(positive | disease) * P(disease) / P(positive).\n2. In that formula, label which factor is the base rate (prevalence) and which is the sensitivity. Write one sentence explaining why they are different quantities and what error happens if you swap them.\n3. Use this changed example (different numbers from any you have seen): A disease has a base rate (prevalence) of 2% in the population. A test has sensitivity 90% (detects disease when present) and specificity 95% (correctly negative when disease absent). A person tests positive. Compute the probability they actually have the disease.\n4. Show your work: compute P(positive) using the law of total probability, then apply Bayes' rule. State the final probability as a percentage rounded to one decimal place.\n5. Check your answer: verify that P(positive) equals the sum of the true-positive and false-positive contributions, and verify that the posterior is between 0 and 1. Write one sentence on whether the result makes sense given the low base rate.\n6. Finally, write two or three sentences explaining how the base rate and sensitivity each affect the posterior in this example, and confirm you did not swap them.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have written Bayes' rule, correctly labeled base rate and sensitivity, computed the posterior for the changed example (base rate 2%, sensitivity 90%, specificity 95%) as approximately 26.9%, shown the total-probability step, checked the result, and explained that you did not swap base rate with sensitivity.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 40,
              "likelyMinutes": 55,
              "highMinutes": 75
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 60,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          },
          {
            "localId": "bayes_remediation",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_core"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2"
            ],
            "title": "Targeted correction of base-rate/sensitivity confusion",
            "activityType": "Failure-triggered remediation",
            "purpose": "If the integrated activity shows the base rate and sensitivity are still swapped or the posterior is wrong, correct that specific step with a focused re-explanation and one corrected computation, without repeating the whole assignment.",
            "instructions": "Only do this if your work on the changed example swapped the base rate and sensitivity or produced an incorrect posterior. Re-read your step 2 labels and your total-probability line. Rewrite the formula with the base rate in the numerator as P(disease) and the sensitivity as P(positive | disease). Then recompute only the posterior for the same example (base rate 2%, sensitivity 90%, specificity 95%), showing the corrected total-probability step. State in one sentence which quantity you had swapped and why the corrected version is right.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have identified the specific swap, rewritten the formula with base rate and sensitivity in the correct positions, and recomputed the posterior correctly for the same example.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 15,
              "likelyMinutes": 20,
              "highMinutes": 30
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 30,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "CONTINGENT",
              "trigger": {
                "checkpointActivityId": "bayes_changed_example",
                "condition": "Your completed work on bayes_changed_example shows the base rate and sensitivity were swapped or the posterior is incorrect."
              }
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "bayes_changed_example"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "A single integrated activity is used instead of separate explanation, practice, and check stages because the scope is small and the changed example itself provides transfer evidence.",
            "The contingent remediation is bounded and failure-triggered so it does not silently extend the assignment when the integrated work already meets the goal."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "Whether the learner can correctly compute the posterior on the first attempt is unknown; the contingent activity handles the case where they cannot."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop after the changed example is correctly explained, computed, and checked, with base rate and sensitivity explicitly distinguished.",
            "If the contingent remediation is triggered, stop after the corrected computation and explanation."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "This plan uses one committed integrated activity (60 minutes) that both explains Bayes' rule during the work and applies it to a changed medical-test example, directly targeting the reported base-rate/sensitivity confusion. A bounded contingent remediation is included only if the integrated work actually shows the confusion persists. No separate practice set or second full check is scheduled because the changed example already provides the transfer evidence, and the scope is small enough that additional work would not add distinct evidence."
}

